humour.character


7. MEDIUM-AWARE FORMATTING

The same joke should not be reused unchanged everywhere.

CARD
- front =3D setup / visual premise
- inside =3D reveal / punchline / personalised tag

T-SHIRT
- one instantly readable concept
- minimal text
- strong silhouette / composition

MUG
- readable from distance
- short line / two-panel loop where useful

POSTER
- larger composition
- fake editorial / news / certificate / art format

SOCIAL VIDEO
- hook
- premise
- escalation
- punchline
- optional tag/callback

STAND-UP
- premise
- setup
- punchline
- tags
- callbacks
- character voice

PET MEME/CARD
- expression/body language
- status inversion
- speech/thought bubble
- owner/pet contrast


8. ROAST.PET V1

Do not launch 50 formats.

Launch 5 canonical formats:

A. PET THOUGHT BUBBLE
Human has a serious/modern-life concern.
Pet has a selfish/absurd trivial concern.

B. PET STAND-UP
Pet at mic/podium roasting owner.

C. BREAKING NEWS
Fake-news visual about owner/pet/occasion.

D. PET THERAPIST / HR
Pet issues diagnosis, warning, performance review, etc.

E. SAVAGE BIRTHDAY
Pet gives recipient personalised birthday truth.

Each template needs:
- fixed layout family
- editable text zones
- photo requirements
- safe print area
- typography rules
- output sizes
- product mappings
- example prompts
- joke constraints
- edge ranges

The brand promise:

=E2=80=9CUpload your pet. We make them say something hilarious.=E2=80=9D

The underlying product:
personalised comedy generation.


9. INITIAL COMMERCE WEDGE

Start with cards.

Reasons:
- fast to understand
- humour-native
- low-friction purchase
- strong gifting urgency
- ideal for personalised copy
- easy to preview
- simpler than apparel
- same art can later expand into shirts, mugs, posters and digital

Initial flow:

User:
=E2=80=9CI need a funny card for Dan tomorrow.=E2=80=9D

Muse:
- asks/uses recipient relationship
- asks for pet photo if relevant
- asks for 2=E2=80=933 useful facts where needed
- offers humour level
- asks permission to use relevant Muse context

Humour Module:
- chooses best format
- generates 3=E2=80=935 variants
- ranks
- outputs composition

Render:
- print-ready art
- digital preview
- optional e-card version

Fulfil:
- Etsy / Moonpig / print provider depending on available connector and
delivery need

Later:
same selected concept =E2=86=92 T-shirt / mug / poster / digital video


10. FULFILMENT ROUTER

Do not overbuild this in FunnyLabs.

Use a thin adapter layer:

commerce.search_products()
commerce.quote()
commerce.create_preview()
commerce.publish()
commerce.order()
commerce.track()

Providers can include:
- Etsy
- Moonpig
- Printify
- Prodigi
- others later

The core humour system should output a provider-neutral product spec.

Example:

{
  "product_type": "greeting_card",
  "design_asset": "...",
  "inside_copy": "...",
  "recipient": "...",
  "delivery_deadline": "...",
  "budget": 25,
  "destination": "...",
  "preferences": {...}
}

Then the router chooses provider based on:
- supported product
- deadline
- geography
- landed cost
- print requirements
- availability


11. FUNNYLABS =E2=80=94 RESEARCH LOOP

FunnyLabs is where the durable learning happens.

Do not jump directly into complex RL.

Start with ranking + contextual bandit style experiments.

For each prompt/context:
1. Generate multiple structurally different candidates
2. Present/select
3. Log outcome
4. Update priors

Capture:
- template ID
- joke structure
- topic
- topicality
- personalisation depth
- edge level
- avatar/persona
- medium
- which candidate was selected
- edits requested
- purchase/no purchase
- share/no share
- replay/watch completion for videos
- explicit rating where available
- =E2=80=9Cmake it harsher/less cringe/shorter=E2=80=9D corrections

Important:
A purchase is a stronger signal than a like.
A share is stronger than passive impression.
A recipient reaction can be stronger than creator approval.
User edits are highly informative negative/positive preference signals.

Build a reward hierarchy rather than one flat score.


12. SOCIAL / AI STAND-UP AS R&D

Social content is not a side hobby.
It is the cheapest high-volume test bench for humour structures.

Use Pogtown-style avatars / recurring synthetic characters.

Run:
- topical monologues
- one-liners
- pet stand-up
- current-event commentary
- character conflict
- crowd-work-like formats where safe/possible
- recurring bits
- callbacks

Measure:
- hold rate
- completion rate
- replays
- comments quoting the joke
- shares
- saves
- follower conversion
- variation performance

The goal is not merely =E2=80=9Cviral content=E2=80=9D.
The goal is to learn:
- which structures create surprise
- which personas can carry which joke types
- which topics combine with which tone
- where AI humour feels fake
- what makes a recurring synthetic comedian feel authored rather than
autogenerated

Winning social structures can become commerce templates.
Winning commerce formats can become recurring social bits.

That loop is key.


13. TIMELY HUMOUR SUPPLY

The engine needs a continuously refreshed topic/template feed.

Inputs:
- current news
- memes
- tech/AGI developments
- football/sport moments
- internet discourse
- seasonal occasions
- workplace/cultural trends

Do not simply summarize news.

Transform news into reusable comedic frames.

Example:
NEWS: new model automates a profession
TEMPLATE: human existential dread / pet unchanged priorities
TEMPLATE: pet performance review of now-redundant owner
TEMPLATE: =E2=80=9Cbreaking news: dog remains household=E2=80=99s only irre=
placeable employee=E2=80=9D

Store:
- source topic
- freshness window
- joke frames
- suitable audiences
- risky references
- expiry

The humour module can then choose current references when the user=E2=80=99=
s
taste permits them.


